Now, when He had indicated the degree of martyrdom, after having mentioned the reward of those who accepted His admonition, even if it meant killing themselves, and had censured those who refused that, after having warned against the enemies from among the People of the Book, the polytheists, and the deceitful hypocrites, the motives of those who desire noble deeds were stirred to look forward to them. He turned to the believers, delighting them with the goodness of His address, calling them to jihad, together with guidance toward preparing for it with what frightens the opposers. So He, glory be to Him and exalted be He, said, alerting them with the particle of distance and the past tense form, indicating that the firmly grounded should not need to be alerted to something like this:

﴿O you who have believed﴾; i.e., who have affirmed faith.

Since He, glory be to Him and exalted be He, had created in man an intellect that moves him to vigilance and to guarding against fear, and it is like an instrument for him, yet because of his frequent heedlessness and forgetfulness in most times he neglects it, it was as if he had abandoned an instrument that was his, He, glory be to Him and exalted be He, said:

﴿Take your precaution﴾; i.e., against the enemies whom I have mentioned to you and whom I have warned you about, the dissenters among them and the hypocrites;

﴿then go forth﴾; i.e., set out, confirming what you have claimed, to fight them, hastening;

﴿in groups﴾; i.e., in separate bands, one expedition after another, never growing weary of this at all;

﴿or go forth all together﴾; i.e., as one army, and do not hold back lest you perish.

It was as if He said: I have lightened for you the killing of souls, in the manner in which I prescribed it for those before you, and I have commanded you only with what you are accustomed to and what you praise one another for and censure whoever abandons, from the arenas of fighting, which are the paths of heroes and the watering places of stallion men; and I have made for the remaining among you the most beloved things of victory and the lawful spoils; and for the one who has passed on, the most beloved of the beloved, which is the degree beyond which there is nothing but the degree of prophethood, even though nothing was diminished from his lifespan; and had he not been killed in that path that is pleasing, he would have been killed in another at that same time.

Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 4, verse 105:

When the first of these stories was the marvelling at the state of those who were given a share of the Book, in their misguidance and in their leading others astray; then the marvelling at their belief in al-Jibt (idols or sorcery) and al-Taghut (false deities); then the marvelling at the state of those who claimed to believe in this Book, along with the earlier Books, and then were content with the judgment of other than it; and He, glorified and exalted, set forth the roots and branches of all of that, established the proofs until they rose above the two Pleiades (al-Farqadan) and their light spread over both horizons (al-Khafiqan), and He concluded that by striving against the falsifiers with argument and with the sword, and crowned all of that with the two attributes of knowledge and wisdom, it was most fitting, in the most perfect fitting, to report that He had sent down this Book with the truth, and to make clear its benefit, which the hypocrites turned away from by their disregard of it. So He said: ﴿Indeed We have sent down to you the Book﴾; that is, by what We have of greatness, which every greatness falls short of; ﴿to you﴾; that is, specifically; and you are the most perfect of creation; ﴿the Book﴾; that is, the complete one, comprising every good; ﴿with the truth﴾; that is, combined with what the reality corresponds to (p-388) ﴿so that you may judge between the people﴾; that is, generally, because your call is general; so there is no one more astray than one who turns away from your judgment and seeks guidance from other than your Book. And He indicated that he does not speak from desire by His saying: ﴿by what Allah has shown you﴾; that is, has made you know, He who has all-encompassing power and perfect knowledge. So if He has made something clear to you with the utmost clarity, then act upon it; and otherwise, await clarification from Him. Then He, glorified and exalted, proceeded to complete what remained of their accounts, to uncover what was hidden of their secrets, and to explain their signs, so that they might be recognized, and that the believers might avoid them, lest they be branded with their brand.

And since He, glorified and exalted, had lightened for him (ﷺ) by prescribing for him contentment in judging by the outward, and not being obligated to probe their inner secrets, by defending Tu'mah ibn Ubayriq; because his affair was problematic, for he had stolen a coat of mail and deposited it with a Jew; it was found in his possession, and he claimed that Tu'mah had deposited it with him; and that was not established against Tu'mah until Allah, glorified and exalted, sent down the verse. So He (exalted) willed to send it down in this incident and others, of what He wills, glorified and exalted, in the Khidrite station (al-maqam al-khidri) of judging by what is in the reality of the matter, which none knows except Allah, glorified and exalted; since the sound opinion on which the majority stands, as transmitted by our shaykh, the judge of the Shafi'is in Egypt, Abu al-Fadl Ahmad ibn Ali ibn Hajar, may Allah have mercy on him (exalted), (p-389) in al-Isabah fi Asma' al-Sahabah, is that al-Khidr, upon him be blessings and peace, was a prophet; and our Prophet (ﷺ) had been given the like of all the miracles of the prophets, may the blessings of Allah be upon them, together with what he was singled out with beyond them all, the most excellent prayers and most complete peace and blessings upon all of them. Then He (exalted) said, linking to what is known to be its implied sense, such as: "So judge by what We show you of the seas of knowledge which We have deposited in this Book": ﴿And do not be for the treacherous ones﴾; that is, on their behalf, from Tu'mah and others; ﴿an advocate﴾; that is, one who disputes on behalf of those who dispute with them;
